### Problem 1: Stop‑and‑Wait Utilisation

A geostationary satellite link has a bandwidth of 2 Mb/s and a one‑way propagation delay of 250 ms.  
Frames are 1250 bytes long. ACK frames are negligible in size.

**(a)** Calculate the transmission time for one data frame.  
**(b)** Determine the total round‑trip time (RTT) assuming processing time is zero.  
**(c)** Using the stop‑and‑wait protocol, what is the maximum effective throughput?  
**(d)** What fraction of the link’s capacity is actually used (the utilisation $U$)?  
**(e)** How many frames would need to be “in flight” (pipelined) to fully utilise the link, assuming a sliding window protocol with window size $W$?

---

**Solution:**

**(a)**
Frame size $L = 1250 \text{ bytes} = 1250 \times 8 = 10,000 \text{ bits}$.  
Transmission time  

$$
t_{\text{tx}} = \frac{L}{R} = \frac{10,000 \text{ bits}}{2 \times 10^6 \text{ bps}} = 0.005 \text{ s} = 5 \text{ ms}.
$$

**(b)**
One‑way delay $t_{\text{prop}} = 250 \text{ ms}$.  
RTT $= 2 \times t_{\text{prop}} = 500 \text{ ms} = 0.5 \text{ s}$.

**(c)**
Stop‑and‑wait sends one frame, then waits for the ACK. The total cycle time per frame is  

$$
t_{\text{cycle}} = t_{\text{tx}} + \text{RTT} = 5 \text{ ms} + 500 \text{ ms} = 505 \text{ ms}.
$$

Effective throughput = $\frac{\text{frame size}}{t_{\text{cycle}}} = \frac{10,000 \text{ bits}}{0.505 \text{ s}} \approx 19,802 \text{ bps}$.

**(d)**
Link capacity $= 2 \text{ Mb/s} = 2,000,000 \text{ bps}$.

$$
U = \frac{t_{\text{tx}}}{t_{\text{tx}} + \text{RTT}} = \frac{5 \text{ ms}}{505 \text{ ms}} \approx 0.0099 \ \ (= 0.99\%).
$$

**(e)**
To keep the pipe full, the window size must be at least the bandwidth‑delay product (BDP) divided by frame size.

$\text{BDP} = R \times \text{RTT} = 2 \times 10^6 \times 0.5 = 1,000,000 \text{ bits} = 125,000 \text{ bytes}$.  
Number of frames needed:

$$
W \ge \frac{\text{BDP}}{L} = \frac{125,000 \text{ bytes}}{1250 \text{ bytes}} = 100 \text{ frames}.
$$

