### Problem 4: Pipelining and Bandwidth‑Delay Product

A terrestrial fibre link runs at 10 Gb/s with a one‑way propagation delay of 15 ms. The sender uses a sliding window protocol with a fixed window size of 5000 frames. Frame size is 1500 bytes. ACKs are 64 bytes.

**(a)** Calculate the bandwidth‑delay product (BDP) in bits and in number of frames.  
**(b)** Does the window size of 5000 fully utilise the link?  
**(c)** What is the maximum throughput achievable with the given window size?  
**(d)** How would the answer change if Selective Repeat is used instead of Go‑Back‑N (assuming no packet loss)?

---

**Solution:**

**(a)**
RTT = 2 × 15 ms = 30 ms = 0.03 s.  
$\text{BDP (bits)} = 10 \times 10^9 \times 0.03 = 300 \times 10^6 \text{ bits} = 300 \text{ Mb}$.  
Frame size $L = 1500 \times 8 = 12,000 \text{ bits}$.  
$\text{BDP (frames)} = \frac{300 \times 10^6}{12,000} = 25,000 \text{ frames}$.

**(b)**
No. A window of 5000 frames can only fill $5000 / 25,000 = 20\%$ of the pipe. The link would be largely idle.

**(c)**
Maximum throughput = $\frac{\text{window size} \times \text{frame size}}{\text{RTT}} = \frac{5000 \times 12,000}{0.03} = 2 \times 10^9 \text{ bps} = 2 \text{ Gb/s}$.

**(d)**
In the absence of losses, both Go‑Back‑N and Selective Repeat deliver the same throughput for a given window size, because the sender can keep the window full. The protocol difference only matters when retransmissions occur. So the answer remains 2 Gb/s.

